ir. But with only two tiles in hand, discarding either leaves you with one tile \u2014 not a legal state unless you draw first. This suggests a misunderstanding.\n\nWait: Standard hand is 13 tiles before drawing. You have:\n- Melds: 3+3+3+3 = 12 tiles\n- Hand: 2 tiles \u2192 total 14? That\u2019s invalid.\n\nAh, correction: After forming four melds (12 tiles), you should have only **one** tile in hand before drawing. But here you have **two**: W6 and T5. That means you must have drawn a tile already and now must discard one.\n\nSo current state: You\u2019ve drawn a tile (now have 13 + 1 = 14 in possession), and must discard one to stay at 13, awaiting winning condition.\n\nBut you don\u2019t have a pair. Your melds are all complete, so the eye (pair) must come from the two single tiles. But W6 and T5 are different. So unless you just drew one of them and now discard the other, you cannot form a pair unless you draw a duplicate of W6 or T5.\n\nThus, you are in a state where you have **no pair**, and your only hope is that you will draw a W6 or T5 to pair up. But currently, after drawing, you have two unmatched tiles, so you must discard one.\n\nTherefore, you must choose which singleton to keep based on:\n- Safety\n- Likelihood of drawing its duplicate\n- Opponent tendencies\n\nUnshown tiles:\n- W6: 4 copies still available \u2014 all four are still in wall/other hands \u2192 high chance someone may need it, but no one has shown W6.\n- T5: 4 copies unshown \u2014 also completely available.\n\nNo one has shown any meld involving W6 or T5.\n\nBut look at discards:\n- Across player has discarded B1, B2, B4, B9 \u2014 seems focused on low Bamboos and mid Dots.\n- Previous player has discarded many B-tiles and T7-T9 \u2014 possibly going for Terminals or mixed hand.\n- Next player discarded T1, T2, T8, T9 \u2014 signs of Terminal focus.\n\nW3-W5 chows: You have two identical ones already exposed. Others may infer you're going for Pure Triple Chow (24 points). But you already have four melds \u2014 so perhaps you completed your hand structure.\n\nBut you lack a pair. So your only viable pairs are W6\u2013W6 or T5\u2013T5. Since you hold one each, you must keep one and discard the other.\n\nWhich to keep?\n\nConsider **tile availability and danger**:\n- W6: 4 still out \u2014 safe to discard? Possibly, since no one has claimed W6-related chows. Previous player discarded W4 three times and W3 once \u2014 they might have completed their hand already. Also, you yourself used W4 in Chi, so others know W4 is active.\n- T5: 4 still out. No one has shown interest in T5. T1-T2-T3 was melded by you, but you used Chi T2 \u2014 so T2 was discarded by previous player.\n\nBut the across player has [B3,B3,B3] and [W7,W7,W7], and discarded B4, B1 \u2014 likely going for Lower Tiles or Half Flush in B or W. They have W7 pung \u2014 so they may want upper Character tiles. W6 is adjacent \u2014 possible wait for W6-W7 chow? But they already have a pung of W7, so likely not building chows around W6.\n\nSimilarly, previous player has [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 upper Bamboo \u2014 unlikely to need T5.\n\nNext player has no melds shown \u2014 dangerous. Could be concealed hand.\n\nBut T5 is central \u2014 more versatile. W6 is safer to keep because:\n- You already have two W3-W4-W5 chows \u2014 keeping W6 could suggest you're shifting to Upper Tiles or Full Flush in Characters, but W6 is not terminal.\n- However, W6 completes a sequence with W5, so if you held W5, it would be useful \u2014 but you don't.\n\nNeither W6 nor T5 helps complete a chow \u2014 you already have full melds.\n\nTherefore, you are simply waiting to pair one of these two.\n\nSince both have equal unshown count (4), and no clear indicator of danger, we assess **discard history**:\n\n- You have already played W7 earlier (Play W7 in history), and W7 is now a pung for the across player \u2014 so they likely needed it. W6 is adjacent \u2014 could they be waiting for W6 to complete a chow? Unlikely, since they have a pung of W7 \u2014 they probably don't need W6.\n- Similarly, T5: previous player has T7-T8-T9 \u2014 no sign of needing T5.\n\nSo both are relatively safe to discard.\n\nBut which one to **keep**?\n\nYou have two identical chows in W3-W4-W5 \u2014 very strong indication of **Pure Triple Chow** (if you had three such chows). But you only have two. To have three, you\u2019d need another W3-W4-W5 \u2014 but you already used all your W3, W4, W5 in two chows? Let's check:\n\nEach [W3,W4,W5] uses one W3, one W4, one W5. Two such chows use: W3\u00d72, W4\u00d72, W5\u00d72.\n\nBut W3: unshown 0 \u2014 all gone. W4: unshown 0 \u2014 all gone. W5: unshown 2 \u2014 two left.\n\nSo W3 and W4 are exhausted \u2014 **impossible** for anyone to make another W3-W4-W5 chow. Therefore, **Pure Triple Chow is impossible** for anyone now.\n\nSo your two chows are just two chows \u2014 not leading to 24-point pattern.\n\nNow reconsider scoring possibilities.\n\nYour melds:\n- [W3,W4,W5] \u00d72 \u2192 two chows in same suit\n- [T1,T2,T3] \u2192 chow in Bamboo\n- [B2,B2,B2] \u2192 pung in Dots\n\nHand: W6, T5\n\nThis is a mixed suit hand, with honors absent.\n\nPossible patterns:\n- **All Chows** (2 points): Yes, if you have four chows and a pair. But you have only three chows and one pung \u2192 currently not All Chows.\n- To get All Chows, you'd need to convert B2 pung into a chow \u2014 impossible.\n\nAlternatively, if you are aiming for **Mixed Shifted Chows** or **Pure Straight**, but you lack the sequences.\n\nGiven the melds, likely you're going for a simple **Chicken Hand** (8 points), which requires only 8 points and no zero-fan.\n\nOther minimal patterns:\n- One Voided Suit? You have W, T, B \u2014 all three suits present \u2192 no.\n- No Honors? Yes \u2014 no honor tiles in hand or melds \u2192 **No Honors (1 point)**.\n- All Simples? Yes \u2014 no terminals (1 or 9) or honors. Check:\n  - W3, W4, W5: all simples\n  - T1, T2, T3: T1 and T3 are terminals \u2192 **not All Simples**\n- T1 and T3 are terminals \u2192 violates All Simples.\n\nSo not All Simples.\n\nBut you have terminal chows: T1-T2-T3 \u2014 contains terminals.\n\nSo possible pattern: **Outside Hand** (4 points) \u2014 every meld contains terminals or honors. Let's see:\n- [W3,W4,W5]: internal \u2014 no terminal (3 is not terminal; 1 and 9 are)\n  - Rule: \"terminals\" = 1 or 9\n  - So W3 not terminal \u2192 this meld is not terminal/honor \u2192 fails Outside Hand\n- [T1,T2,T3]: contains T1 and T3 \u2014 terminals \u2192 ok\n- [B2,B2,B2]: B2 \u2014 not terminal \u2192 not valid for Outside Hand\n\nSo not Outside Hand.\n\nBack to Chicken Hand \u2014 likely candidate.\n\nYou need 8 points. Currently, possible fans:\n- **One Voided Suit?** No \u2014 three suits.\n- **No Honors?** Yes \u2014 1 point\n- **Melded Hand?** No \u2014 your chows were formed by Chi, so exposed, but you didn't take the winning tile yet. If you win by claiming a discard, and all melds are exposed, then yes. But you haven't won yet.\n\nBut you have:\n- Two chows formed by **Chi W4** (you did Chi W4 twice) \u2014 so exposed\n- One chow formed by **Chi T2** \u2014 exposed\n- One pung formed by **Peng B2** \u2014 exposed\n\nSo all melds are exposed \u2192 **Melded Hand** (6 points) is achievable if you win by claiming a discard.\n\nThen you need 2 more points.\n\nWhat else?\n- **All Pungs?** No \u2014 you have chows.\n- **All Chows?** You have three chows and one pung \u2192 not All Chows.\n\nUnless the [B2,B2,B2] is considered a pung, so not chow-based.\n\nSo not All Chows.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is there a possibility of **Mixed Double Chow** (1 point)? You have two identical chows: [W3,W4,W5] \u00d72 \u2014 same suit, same numbers \u2192 this qualifies for **Pure Double Chow** (1 point).\n\nYes! **Pure Double Chow** \u2014 two identical chows in the same suit \u2192 1 point.\n\nAnd you have that.\n\nAlso, **No Honors** \u2014 1 point.\n\nTotal so far: 1 (Pure Double Chow) + 1 (No Honors) = 2 points.\n\nBut you need 8.\n\nMelded Hand: 6 points \u2014 if achieved.\n\nTotal: 2 + 6 = 8 \u2192 exactly Chicken Hand level.\n\nSo if you win by claiming a discard (making it Melded Hand), you can reach 8 points via:\n- Melded Hand (6)\n- Pure Double Chow (1)\n- No Honors (1)\n\nTotal: 8 \u2192 valid win.\n\nTherefore, you are in a position to win as soon as you get a pair and claim a discard.\n\nCurrently, you have W6 and T5 \u2014 no pair.\n\nYou must keep one and discard the other.\n\nYou need to draw a duplicate of whichever you keep.\n\nWhich tile is more likely to be discarded by others?\n\nLook at recent discards:\n- Previous player: last few plays: Play B9, Play F4, Play B5, Play J1, Play B2, Play W4, Play J1, Play B5, Play F4, Play B9 \u2014 heavy on B, F, J, W4 \u2014 W4 is gone, but W6 hasn't been discarded recently.\n- Across player: Play T7, J1, T7, J1, F3, B1, B4, B2 \u2014 no W6 or T5.\n- Next player: Play T9, B8, B5, W2, T8, W2 \u2014 no T5 or W6.\n\nNo one is discarding W6 or T5.\n\nBut T5 is more central and commonly used in straights \u2014 higher chance someone might discard it later.\n\nW6: you have already discarded W7, and across player Peng-ed W7 \u2014 they likely wanted it for pung, not for chow. So W6 is less likely to be needed.\n\nBut W6 could be useful for Full Flush in W if someone is going for it.\n\nStill, both are medium danger.\n\nBut consider: **you already have two W3-W4-W5 chows** \u2014 your hand screams \"Characters\" \u2014 opponents may avoid discarding Character tiles to you.\n\nWhereas T5 is in Bamboo, and you only have one chow in Bamboo (T1-T2-T3), and you Chi-ed T2 \u2014 which was discarded by previous player \u2014 so they know you're collecting Bamboo.\n\nBut T5 is not near T1-T2-T3 \u2014 gap \u2014 so less obvious.\n\nStill, holding T5 might be safer long-term.\n\nBut for pairing: which tile is more likely to appear?\n\nBoth have 4 copies unshown \u2014 full set.\n\nBut W3, W4 are exhausted \u2014 suggests many players are using middle Characters \u2014 so W5, W6 might be in demand.\n\nW5 has 2 unshown \u2014 some are out.\n\nBut no melds show W5.\n\nGiven that, W6 might be hotter.\n\nT5: no activity \u2014 cold tile.\n\nSo **T5 is safer to keep** \u2014 less likely to help others.\n\nMoreover, if you keep T5, and someone discards it later, you can win.\n\nBut you need a pair \u2014 so you must draw T5 or W6.\n\nSince the wall is thin (many tiles discarded), probability is low.\n\nBut you have to choose.\n\nAnother factor: **suit diversity**.\n\nIf you keep T5, you maintain presence in three suits \u2014 better for **All Types**? But you lack honors \u2014 you have no honor melds.\n\nTo get All Types (8 points), you need all three suits, winds, dragons.\n\nYou have no wind or dragon melds.\n\nBut you have discarded F1, F3, F4 \u2014 so others know you had winds.\n\nBut you don't have a wind pung.\n\nSo not Big Three Winds, etc.\n\nNot relevant.\n\nFinal decision:\n\nKeep **T5**, discard **W6**.\n\nWhy?\n- W6 is in the same suit as your multiple chows \u2014 opponents may suspect you're completing a flush or shifted chows.\n- T5 is isolated, less suspicious.\n- Discarding W6: you've already used W3-W4-W5, and W6 is adjacent, so could be mistaken for a wait on W5-W6-W7 or W6-W7-W8 \u2014 dangerous.\n- T5 is farther from your T1-T2-T3 chow \u2014 safer to keep.\n\nBut you must discard one.\n\nAnswer: Play W6.\n\nEvaluation: Both W6 and T5 are safe discards, but W6 is in the same suit as two existing chows, making it slightly more suspicious if kept. T5 is in a suit with only one chow and is less likely to be seen as part of a major pattern. Keeping T5 offers a marginally safer path toward forming a pair without signaling a strong hand. Additionally, the lack of activity around T5 makes it a cold tile, reducing risk when kept.\n\nReason: Discarding W6 reduces the visibility of my focus on the Character suit, while keeping T5 maintains a low-profile wait for a pair. This minimizes the chance of alerting opponents to a potential win while preserving flexibility.\n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
